---
/* Run 6, concept C: Hands-on (lib/build.ts). The visitor feels the product in the first second.
     1. The hero is a live toy: hold the key (fn if the browser reports it, else the space bar) or the big button,
        and the app's pill listens; let go, and a sample sentence lands in the note (Hero.astro, toy.ts).
     2. The features are one bento grid where every tile is a small demo you can touch (Bento.astro, demos.ts).
     3. Getting started and the price, side by side, both touchable (Get.astro).
     4. The short FAQ and the last call to action (Faq.astro).
   The motion is tactile, not scripted: keys go down, the pill grows and glows while it listens, text lands with a
   short highlight, and nothing moves on its own except the slow liquid surfaces (one per screen). Every tile's HTML
   is its finished state, so the page reads completely without JavaScript; with reduced motion nothing loops and every
   change is a plain swap. The page runs no React: the design system's components render to static HTML here, and
   two small vanilla modules make them react. Everything this concept adds is prefixed ho- (hands-on.css). */
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Bento from "./Bento.astro";
import Faq from "./Faq.astro";
import Get from "./Get.astro";
import Hero from "./Hero.astro";
import "./hands-on.css";
---
<script is:inline>document.documentElement.classList.add("ho-js")</script>
<Header current="home" />
<main id="indhold" class="ho">
  <Hero />
  <Bento />
  <Get />
  <Faq />
</main>
<script>
  import { demos } from "./demos";
  import { toy } from "./toy";

  const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-toy]");
  if (root) toy(root);
  demos();
</script>
